      id: "mw-090",
      part: "p4",
      chapter: 90,
      chapterTitle: "Martin’s Very Big Day",
      pages: "200–201",
      title: "Martin's Very Big Day: small choices you can't take back",
      minutes: 6,
      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "Magical Worlds of the Wizard of Ads (Roy H. Williams, 2001)", section: "Chapter 90 · Martin’s Very Big Day · pp. 200–201", who: "Roy H. Williams" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "Nine years later, Martin acknowledged Columbus as the true discoverer and immediately dropped the name “America” from his maps. But by then it was too late.", page: "200" },
        { text: "Martin’s decision affected only the name of a country, a word on a map. Our decisions affect the lives of human beings.", page: "200–201" }
      ],
      idea: `Roy sets a trap. His name was Martin, and in the early 1500s he wrote something that changed the world forever. You think Martin Luther and the Ninety-Five Theses of 1517. Wrong Martin. This one did his writing ten years, six months and six days earlier. On April 25, 1507, mapmaker **Martin Waldseemüller** wrote the word **America** on a continent that had no name.

His world map was the first to show the New World as two continents joined by a narrow strait. He named the southern one after Amerigo Vespucci, whom he believed had discovered it, and explained the choice in a book that accompanied the map. Nine years later he decided Columbus deserved the credit and dropped "America" from his maps. Too late. The name had already spread, and it stuck.

Then Roy turns it around. Martin's decision, world-famous as it is, affected only a word on a map. **Our everyday decisions affect people.** We encourage someone, and help shape their success. We repeat gossip, and open a breach that may never heal. We give time or money, and make the world a little better. Today we'll choose whether to frown or smile, hide love or show it, encourage or discourage. Each of those choices, Roy argues, will shape the future more than a mistaken mapmaker's label. Toynbee's sidebar: our destiny isn't predetermined; we determine it.

There are two lessons here: one Roy states, one he demonstrates.
- **Stated:** small personal choices are the truly big decisions, because they land on human beings.
- **Demonstrated:** once a name or a story is out in the world, you can't recall it. Martin's correction didn't matter; the first version won. Words, once released, have a life of their own. (That's why the gossip example belongs here.)

> **Principle:** Act as if every small word lasts, because it might. You can't recall a name, a rumor or an encouragement once it's out.

**For ZDub:**
- **Branding:** the first name you give an offer or campaign in public tends to stick, so name things on purpose before launch. Like Martin, you won't get to rename it later without confusion.
- **People:** a reply telling a nervous PMT prospect "you've got this," a quick note praising a client's front-desk person, refusing to trash a competitor: these are Zach's "very big days." They're also how a local reputation gets built, one person at a time.`,
      keyPoints: [
        "The Martin who changed the world in 1507 was mapmaker Waldseemüller, who named America after Amerigo Vespucci.",
        "He later credited Columbus and dropped the name, but it had already stuck.",
        "Our daily choices (encourage or discourage, gossip or not, give or not) land on people, so they matter more than a map label.",
        "Names and words, once released, can't be recalled.",
        "We determine our destiny through our choices (Toynbee)."
      ],
      why: `ZDub's reputation in a local market is built from small daily choices (every comment, every email, every name it gives an offer), and none of them can be recalled once they spread.`,
      example: {
        title: "Naming a PMT offer as if it's permanent (because it is)",
        before: `"We'll launch it as 'The Winter Promo' this week and come up with a better name later."`,
        after: `"Before launch, Zach and Chris pick a name they'd be happy hearing customers repeat for years: 'First Ride Weekend.' It says what it is, sounds like an experience, and still works in July."`,
        body: `Martin learned that a correction can't catch a name that's already traveling. A placeholder name on an ad becomes the name customers use, so spend ten minutes choosing it on purpose. The same goes for words about people: a reply to a nervous prospect in PMT's comments ("Plenty of students are scared the first morning. You'll be fine.") is a tiny choice that lands on a real person.`
      },
